{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fd6047055a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-328","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Glenrock Museum is in eastern Poland. It opened in 1995.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 381,506 visitors.</li><li>The Brookhaven Museum is in western Scotland. It opened in 1911.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 295,790 visitors.</li><li>Both are museums of natural history.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Glenrock Museum and the Brookhaven Museum.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Glenrock Museum opened in 1995, while the Brookhaven Museum opened in 1911.","The annual attendance of the Glenrock Museum (381,506 visitors) is greater than that of the Brookhaven Museum (295,790 visitors).","The Glenrock Museum is in eastern Poland, and the Brookhaven Museum is in western Scotland.","Both the Glenrock Museum and the Brookhaven Museum are museums of natural history."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f79390e19c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-334","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Dunmore Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1943.</li><li>Its height is 503 meters.</li><li>The Larkspur Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1971.</li><li>Its height is 116 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two towers differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Dunmore Tower (503 meters) is greater than that of the Larkspur Tower (116 meters).","The Dunmore Tower, which is in eastern Australia, is one of several towers built mainly of steel.","Both the Dunmore Tower and the Larkspur Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The Dunmore Tower was completed in 1943, while the Larkspur Tower was completed in 1971."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both towers. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one tower."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.679716ea76","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-340","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Merrow is in southern Norway. It was first climbed in 1921.</li><li>Its height is 6,079 meters.</li><li>Mount Larkspur is in northern Japan. It was first climbed in 1989.</li><li>Its height is 3,167 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the height of the two mountains differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Mount Merrow and Mount Larkspur are peaks in the same mountain range.","The height of Mount Merrow (6,079 meters) is greater than that of Mount Larkspur (3,167 meters).","Mount Merrow, which is in southern Norway, is one of several peaks in the same mountain range.","Mount Merrow was first climbed in 1921, while Mount Larkspur was first climbed in 1989."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the height of both mountains. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one mountain."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b8e2355527","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-346","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Sable is in central Spain. It was first climbed in 1950.</li><li>Its height is 3,085 meters.</li><li>Mount Cresthill is in western Kenya. It was first climbed in 1987.</li><li>Its height is 1,838 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Mount Sable and Mount Cresthill are dormant volcanoes.","Mount Sable was first climbed in 1950, while Mount Cresthill was first climbed in 1987.","The height of Mount Sable (3,085 meters) is greater than that of Mount Cresthill (1,838 meters).","Mount Sable is in central Spain, and Mount Cresthill is in western Kenya."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7eecb2aefb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-351","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Eastwick National Park is in central Canada. It was established in 1969.</li><li>Its area is 6,719 square kilometers.</li><li>Wexford National Park is in eastern Australia. It was established in 1962.</li><li>Its area is 6,346 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two parks were established.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Eastwick National Park was established in 1969, while Wexford National Park was established in 1962.","Eastwick National Park is in central Canada, and Wexford National Park is in eastern Australia.","Both Eastwick National Park and Wexford National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests.","The area of Eastwick National Park (6,719 square kilometers) is greater than that of Wexford National Park (6,346 square kilometers)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.f1d1c49c42","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-362","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Glenrock is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1916.</li><li>Its surface area is 848 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Cresthill is in northern Japan. It was first surveyed in 2011.</li><li>Its surface area is 97 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Lake Glenrock and Lake Cresthill.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Glenrock is in southern Norway, and Lake Cresthill is in northern Japan.","Both Lake Glenrock and Lake Cresthill are freshwater lakes formed by glaciers.","The surface area of Lake Glenrock (848 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Cresthill (97 square kilometers).","Lake Glenrock was first surveyed in 1916, while Lake Cresthill was first surveyed in 2011."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b4987670b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-366","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1905.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Orla Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1926.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Pembrook Observatory and the Orla Observatory.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Pembrook Observatory began operating in 1905, while the Orla Observatory began operating in 1926.","The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Pembrook Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Orla Observatory (8 meters).","Both the Pembrook Observatory and the Orla Observatory are radio observatories.","The Pembrook Observatory is in southern New Zealand, and the Orla Observatory is in western Kenya."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d80895883c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-369","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Valewood National Park is in eastern Poland. It was established in 1978.</li><li>Its area is 8,644 square kilometers.</li><li>Thornbury National Park is in southern New Zealand. It was established in 1894.</li><li>Its area is 5,011 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two parks were established.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Valewood National Park is in eastern Poland, and Thornbury National Park is in southern New Zealand.","Both Valewood National Park and Thornbury National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests.","The area of Valewood National Park (8,644 square kilometers) is greater than that of Thornbury National Park (5,011 square kilometers).","Valewood National Park was established in 1978, while Thornbury National Park was established in 1894."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b8d0da4a22","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-370","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Fenwick is in western Scotland. It was first climbed in 2011.</li><li>Its height is 3,274 meters.</li><li>Mount Sable is in southern Brazil. It was first climbed in 1999.</li><li>Its height is 2,694 meters.</li><li>Both are dormant volcanoes.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of Mount Fenwick (3,274 meters) is greater than that of Mount Sable (2,694 meters).","Mount Fenwick is in western Scotland, and Mount Sable is in southern Brazil.","Mount Fenwick was first climbed in 2011, while Mount Sable was first climbed in 1999.","Both Mount Fenwick and Mount Sable are dormant volcanoes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ffef9bfce5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-378","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Mount Ironwood is in central Spain. It was first climbed in 1956.</li><li>Its height is 6,389 meters.</li><li>Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland. It was first climbed in 1935.</li><li>Its height is 2,655 meters.</li><li>Both are peaks in the same mountain range.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two mountains were first climbed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mount Ironwood is in central Spain, and Mount Glenrock is in eastern Poland.","The height of Mount Ironwood (6,389 meters) is greater than that of Mount Glenrock (2,655 meters).","Mount Ironwood was first climbed in 1956, while Mount Glenrock was first climbed in 1935.","Both Mount Ironwood and Mount Glenrock are peaks in the same mountain range."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3c77c3dd8b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-8","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Sato is an ecologist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Sato compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Sato found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Ecologist Lucía Sato set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.","To investigate the question, Sato compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Sato found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Sato concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.fdfb90a2cb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-17","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Nakamura is a psychologist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Nakamura had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Nakamura found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Nakamura found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","To investigate the question, Nakamura had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Because students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly, Nakamura concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d3316ba6b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-30","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Nakamura is an entomologist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Nakamura set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Nakamura found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","Entomologist Yara Nakamura set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","To investigate the question, Nakamura set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Nakamura found that bees visited the blue feeders most often."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9a2664c05f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-38","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Solberg is a sociologist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.</li><li>Solberg surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.</li><li>Solberg found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.</li><li>Solberg concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Solberg surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.","Solberg concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.","Solberg found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.","Sociologist Sofia Solberg set out to learn whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8c210ff91d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-56","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Beatriz Haddad is a zoologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.</li><li>Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.</li><li>Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day.</li><li>Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Haddad gave puzzle feeders to some elephants and standard food trays to others, then tracked their movement.","Zoologist Beatriz Haddad set out to learn whether zoo elephants that are given puzzle feeders are more active.","Haddad concluded that puzzle feeders may encourage healthy activity.","Haddad found that elephants with puzzle feeders walked noticeably more each day."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0d593f9b11","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-63","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Maren Brennan is an astronomer.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Brennan measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Brennan found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Brennan found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","To investigate the question, Brennan measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Astronomer Maren Brennan set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.","Brennan concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0119d0a0c3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-67","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Petrov is a linguist.</li><li>Petrov wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Petrov gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Petrov found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Petrov concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Petrov concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Petrov found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","To investigate the question, Petrov gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Linguist Yara Petrov set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d25e5e67b2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-93","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Sato is a hydrologist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Sato compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Sato found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Sato concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Hydrologist Elias Sato set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Sato found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Sato concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Sato compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d1ce637742","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-94","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Halvorsen is a hydrologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Hydrologist Samuel Halvorsen set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e4b3a2ebbf","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-94","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Halvorsen is a hydrologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Halvorsen compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Samuel Halvorsen set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Halvorsen found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","Halvorsen concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a28b7fcddb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-125","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Okafor is an ornithologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Okafor measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Okafor found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Okafor concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Okafor measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Ornithologist Felipe Okafor set out to learn whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.","Okafor found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","Okafor concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3bfaa9e438","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-127","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Duarte is a public health researcher.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Duarte counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Duarte found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Duarte counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Public health researcher Kofi Duarte set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.","Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Duarte found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.18218dae67","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-127","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Duarte is a public health researcher.</li><li>Duarte wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Duarte counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Duarte found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year, Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Duarte concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","Duarte found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","To investigate the question, Duarte counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8bd9b26d1d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-135","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Solberg is an archaeologist.</li><li>Solberg wanted to find out whether a coastal settlement traded with inland villages.</li><li>Solberg analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement.</li><li>Solberg found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.</li><li>Solberg concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because many pots were made from clay found only far inland, Solberg concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Solberg concluded that the settlement may have traded with inland communities.","Solberg found that many pots were made from clay found only far inland.","To investigate the question, Solberg analyzed the minerals in pottery found at the settlement."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ca3780df1e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-147","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Haddad is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Marine ecologist Amara Haddad set out to learn whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.","To investigate the question, Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a267c2340c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-147","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Haddad is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Haddad fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Haddad found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Because kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser, Haddad concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7e5104b6c7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-148","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kwame Ferreira is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Ferreira fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Ferreira found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Because kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser, Ferreira concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Ferreira concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Ferreira fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.","Ferreira found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c3b2e902b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-154","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Mensah is a climate scientist.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Mensah compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Mensah found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Mensah concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Climate scientist Ingrid Mensah set out to learn whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.","Mensah concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Mensah found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Mensah compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.08461fbd4e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-156","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Behavioral economist Elias Takeda set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ea74e8f640","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-168","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Viktor Kowalski is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Kowalski added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Kowalski found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Kowalski concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","Because the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks, Kowalski concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Kowalski added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cb6e64c74d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-173","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Maren Iyer is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Iyer added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Iyer found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Iyer concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Iyer concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Iyer added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Iyer found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.","Because the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks, Iyer concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6904282e3a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-178","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ingrid Mensah is a music researcher.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Mensah tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Mensah found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Mensah found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","To investigate the question, Mensah tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Because children who took lessons improved more than those who did not, Mensah concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."}
